{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "94",
    "case_summary": "A 41-year-old male presented with a 26-year history of issues affecting the right forearm and hand. At age 15, he had surgery on his right middle finger, but recently experienced increased pain and worsening contractures. The second finger of his right hand had become deformed, leading to proximal interphalangeal arthrodesis to alleviate pain and correct the deformity. He had a history of gradual tooth loss starting early in life, culminating in complete tooth loss by age 20. Seven years later, he developed sensorineural deafness in the right ear. He sustained a fracture of the distal right humerus following moderate trauma. Family history was non-contributory. Physical examination revealed short stature (-2SD), curly/kinky hair, and frontal bossing. Oral examination showed anodontia and dimpling over the chin. He was deaf in the right ear with normal hearing in the left ear. He exhibited upper thoracic kyphosis and a unilateral deformity of the right forearm and hand, which was firm and painful to palpation without discoloration, thickening, or increased temperature of the overlying skin. Bilateral bowing of the forearms and legs was observed. A 4-inch hyperpigmented patch with freckling spots was noted on the left side of the trunk, with no additional freckling in the axillae or elsewhere. Ophthalmological and cardiological examinations were normal, and his intelligence was within normal limits.\n\nRadiographic examinations are shown in figures. Blood biochemistry revealed moderate hypercholestrolaemia, and the triglycerides showed mild elevated levels. Serum calcium, phosphorus, and alkaline phosphatase levels were normal. Endocrinological examination of the thyroid hormones, PTH, and adrenocorticotropic hormones showed no abnormalities. Genomic DNA was extracted from the whole blood samples and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) was performed to amplify the 13 exons and flanking intronic regions of the LEMD3-Gene. The PCR products were analysed by direct sequencing. We were unable to detect any mutations in the 13 exons and flanking intronic regions of the LEMD3-gene in our patient.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
